  <a href="#section-choose-diagnostic" id="section-choose-diagnostic" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose a diagnostic tool</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>Choose by the question you need to answer. Preparing a message, running an application's send flow, and inspecting an SMTP connection reveal different information.</p>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Diagnostic tools by question" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">What you get</th><th scope="col">Connects to SMTP?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Will this email pass preparation?</td><td><a href="/debugging.html#section-email-validation"><code>mailer.validate(email)</code></a></td><td>Success or an exception from the same preparation used by full rehearsal</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What message will this Mailer prepare?</td><td><a href="#section-rehearse-message"><code>mailer.rehearse(email)</code></a></td><td>A snapshot with EML bytes, encoded size, effective Email, Message-ID and envelope addresses</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Does my application's send flow behave correctly without delivery?</td><td><a href="#section-debug-mode">Logging-only mode</a></td><td>Email and MIME logs, plus normal send completion and observer callbacks; outcomes identify logging-only mode</td><td>No, for simulated sends</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Which messages does my application submit, so I can capture or assert them?</td><td><a href="/configuration.html#section-custom-mailer">A capturing <code>CustomMailer</code></a></td><td>Your implementation records invocations and the supplied Email and MIME message</td><td>No, when your implementation only captures</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Can the configured connection path open?</td><td><a href="/debugging.html#section-connection-test"><code>mailer.sync().testConnection()</code></a></td><td>Success or an exception, including configured authentication and connection cleanup</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What did this endpoint advertise and negotiate?</td><td><a href="#section-smtp-capabilities"><code>mailer.sync().probeConnection()</code></a></td><td>A report with available greeting, capability, TLS and failure details; authentication is opt-in</td><td>Yes, on a fresh dedicated connection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where did a loaded setting come from?</td><td><a href="#section-config-diagnostics"><code>mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code></a></td><td>Resolved configuration values and their sources, with sensitive values redacted</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What happened to an actual send?</td><td><a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">Submission receipt and mail-send outcome</a></td><td>Available server responses, recipient results and the send's success or failure</td><td>The send does</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What does this received delivery notification say?</td><td><a href="/delivery-reports.html"><code>Email.getDeliveryStatusReport()</code></a></td><td>Reported recipient actions, status codes and correlation facts after ordinary MIME/EML conversion; your application supplies and trusts the mail</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where did a slow or failed send spend its time?</td><td><a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings"><code>outcome.getDiagnostics()</code></a> in the observer</td><td>Available per-phase measurements and where a failure was observed</td><td>No additional connection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What is this email doing right now?</td><td><a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-live-progress"><code>MailSendHandlers</code> progress observer</a></td><td>Latest work, supported greeting/TLS/authentication/envelope stages, retry attempt and upload counts; final results remain authoritative</td><td>No additional connection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>How does mail sending fit into my application's traces and metrics?</td><td><a href="/opentelemetry.html"><code>withTelemetryConfig(...)</code></a></td><td>Logical sends, actual submission attempts and available timing measurements through your own OpenTelemetry instance</td><td>No additional connection</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The connection rows describe the built-in SMTP path. Both calls also have async equivalents and intentionally connect even in logging-only mode.
        With a <code>CustomMailer</code>, <code>testConnection()</code> delegates to your implementation and <code>probeConnection()</code> reports that inspection is unsupported.
        Neither a successful connection test nor a successful probe proves that a particular email will be accepted or reach an inbox.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


<a href="#section-email-validation" id="section-email-validation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Validate and rehearse a message{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <h3>Check addresses and message preparation</h3>
      <p>Run <code>validate</code> in a test for a new receipt email. It returns normally if this Mailer can prepare the message and throws if
        preparation fails. It applies defaults and overrides, builds MIME, runs configured protection and checks the encoded size without opening SMTP
        or changing the original <code>Email</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailer.validate(receiptEmail); // throws if this Mailer cannot prepare it</code></pre>
      <p>For an address by itself, use <code>JMail.isValid(address)</code>. Simple Java Mail uses
        <a href="https://github.com/RohanNagar/jmail">JMail</a> for its configurable address checks, including
        <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2822">RFC 2822</a> syntax.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Customize address validation</summary>
        <p>Use <code>ValidationConfig</code> to change the address policy. <code>clearEmailValidator()</code> removes only that policy.
          Required sender and recipient checks, encoded-word protection and CRLF injection scanning stay active.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ValidationConfig.Builder validation = ValidationConfig.builder()
    .withEmailValidator(JMail.strictValidator()
        .requireOnlyTopLevelDomains(TopLevelDomain.DOT_COM)
        .withRule(email -&gt; email.localPart().startsWith("allowed")));
currentMailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());

// Keep required fields, encoded-word protection and CRLF checks only:
currentMailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.clearEmailValidator().build());
// Restore the default strict JMail address validator:
currentMailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.resetEmailValidator().build());</code></pre>
        <p><code>withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true)</code> reports client-side findings as warnings so SMTP handling can continue.
          Use this when your application leaves the acceptance decision to the server.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">ValidationConfig.Builder validation = currentMailerBuilder.getValidationConfig().toBuilder()
    .withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true);
currentMailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.build());

// Restore blocking validation:
currentMailerBuilder.withValidationConfig(validation.resetClientValidationWarningsOnly().build());</code></pre>
        <p><code>MailerHelper.validate(email)</code> checks the Email directly, without applying Mailer defaults or overrides. For more
          address rules and examples, see <a href="https://github.com/RohanNagar/jmail">JMail</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="section-rehearse-message">
    <div class="wide">
      <h3>Rehearse and save a preview</h3>
      <p>You’ve added a signed PDF to the receipt. Rehearse it to open the prepared message in a mail client, check its encoded size and see
        which envelope addresses the Mailer would pass to the transport. A full rehearsal runs the same checks as <code>validate</code>,
        including any configured S/MIME, OpenPGP or DKIM processing.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">MailRehearsal preview = mailer.rehearse(receiptEmail);
Files.write(Path.of("receipt-preview.eml"), preview.getEmlBytes());
System.out.println("Encoded bytes: " + preview.getEncodedSize());
System.out.println("Envelope recipients: " + preview.getEnvelopeRecipients());</code></pre>
      <p><code>MailRehearsal</code> is an immutable snapshot with the effective Email, a copy of the EML bytes, encoded size, Message-ID and
        envelope sender and recipients. Sending the original Email later prepares it again, so dates, boundaries, IDs and cryptographic output can differ.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure" id="rehearsal-recipient-addresses">
        <summary>Envelope overrides and routes</summary>
        <p><code>getEnvelopeRecipients()</code> lists the immutable transport addresses in order. A named
          group such as <code>Team:alice@example.org,bob@example.org;</code> can occupy one entry. If you redirected the message to a test inbox
          using <code>withOverrideReceivers(...)</code>, those are the addresses you’ll see.</p>
        <p>Typed overrides receive the configured address validation, encoded-word protection and name/address injection checks after defaults
          and overrides. Raw custom <code>To</code>, <code>Cc</code> and <code>Bcc</code> retain the mailbox-validation escape hatch. General injection
          checks, mandatory locks and warnings-only settings still apply.</p>
        <p>Rehearsal uses the Mailer you call. It leaves cluster and failover route selection to the actual send, which may use a Mailer with
          different defaults, overrides or security settings.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure" id="rehearsal-after-close">
        <summary>Preparing after close, base MIME and exact EML</summary>
        <p><code>rehearse(...)</code> and <code>validate(...)</code> work while the Mailer is closing and after it closes.
          Open preparations use its effective Session settings. Later calls use the preparation settings retained when sending shutdown began,
          even if another Mailer reuses the Session. Standalone preparation leaves sending-resource cleanup free to proceed. Keep borrowed
          attachments available until preparation finishes.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailer.close(); // release sending resources
MailRehearsal previewAfterClose = mailer.rehearse(receiptEmail);</code></pre>
        <p><code>mailer.rehearse(email, false)</code> applies defaults and overrides and checks the base MIME without security processing or the
          final size check. For <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">Exact EML</a>, validation checks the explicit envelope and
          parseable canonical EML. Full preparation also checks the supplied byte length. Rehearsal returns those same authoritative bytes.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-connection-test" id="section-connection-test" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Testing a server connection{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        If you just want to do a connection test using your current configuration, including transport strategy and (authenticated)
        proxy, Simple Java Mail got you covered.
      </p>
      <p>
        The connection test can also be done asynchronously and the result can be handled asynchronously as well. Take a look at
        <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-async-results">Handling asynchronous mailing result</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose <code>mailer.sync().testConnection()</code> for a blocking health check, or
        <code>mailer.async().testConnection()</code> for a future. Both include connection cleanup before reporting completion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>// configure your mailer
Mailer mailer = ...;

// perform connection test
mailer.sync().testConnection(); // throws failures directly
CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; test = mailer.async().testConnection();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

<a href="#section-maximum-emailsize" id="section-maximum-emailsize" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
	{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 core=true}}Limit the maximum email size{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

	<section>
        <p class="wide">
          Do you know your server's maximum allowed email size? Then it might be helpful to have Simple Java Mail reject
			emails that exceed this before trying to send them.
        </p>
    <hr/>
		<p class="wide">
			The following throws <code>EmailTooBigException</code> directly. It belongs to the <code>MailException</code> family and has kind
			<code>MESSAGE_SIZE</code>, so either a specific catch or your common library-failure handler can inspect it.
		</p>
		<p class="wide">
			For <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">exact EML</a>, the measured size is the supplied wire-byte length; Simple Java Mail does not rebuild the
			message merely to measure it.
		</p>
        <div class="wide">
		    <pre><code>Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
      .(..)
      .withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
          .withMaximumEmailSize(4) // 4 bytes, that's not much
          .build())
      .buildMailer();

try {
	mailer.sync().sendMail(emailBiggerThan4Bytes);
} catch (EmailTooBigException e) {
	// failure: EmailTooBigException (kind MESSAGE_SIZE)
	// msg: "Email size of 277 bytes exceeds maximum allowed size of 4 bytes"
	e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
      </div>
	</section>

  <a href="#section-debug-mode" id="section-debug-mode" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Logging emails instead of sending</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Run your application’s usual send calls in QA and inspect what they prepare. A logging-only Mailer writes the Email and MIME
      at <code>INFO</code> and skips SMTP submission. Preparation, async scheduling, completion handling and result observers still run.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withTransportModeLoggingOnly(true)
        .buildMailer()) {
    MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
    System.out.println(receipt.getStatus());
}</code></pre>

    <p>For a successful simulated send, look for the prepared MIME in the log and <code>UNKNOWN</code> in the printed receipt.
      The receipt includes the intended recipients. <code>MailSendOutcome.isLoggingOnly()</code> identifies the simulation in your observer.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Enable through properties</summary>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.transport.mode.logging.only=true</code></pre>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Recipient results and which callbacks run</summary>
<p>Configured total send deadlines and <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> callbacks still apply.
      The prepared content is normally logged at <code>TRACE</code>.</p>      <p>
        The logging-only receipt lists the prepared MIME recipients in order. Explicit override receivers supply the delivery list when present,
        and duplicate occurrences remain distinct. A custom-mailer receipt describes the same intent before the callback. Both modes leave
        acceptance <code>UNKNOWN</code> and SMTP replies absent. If the prepared MIME recipient headers cannot be parsed, the receipt uses the
        typed recipients or the explicit override receivers.
      </p>

      <p>For a preview or a captured message in a test, see <a href="#section-choose-diagnostic">the diagnostic tool comparison</a>.
        For live connection checks, use <code>testConnection()</code> or <code>probeConnection()</code>.</p>
</details>

  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-override-receivers" id="section-override-receivers" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Override envelope-level receivers</h2>

  <section>
	  <div class="view">
		  <p>
			  For testing purposes, you can change where the email is sent to, without touching the actual email being sent, by overriding the envelope-level
			  receivers. This keeps test routing outside the message content while preserving the original addresses for inspection.
		  </p>
		  <p>These typed delivery destinations follow the selected Mailer's address validator and relevant injection checks during validation,
			  rehearsal and sending, including destinations supplied by defaults or overrides. Their order, repeated occurrences and recipient
			  metadata remain intact. The <a href="/email-composition.html#section-custom-headers">custom headers reference</a> covers how generic
			  <code>To</code>, <code>Cc</code> and <code>Bcc</code> setters populate the visible recipient fields.</p>
		  <p>
			  Copies made with <code>mail.emailBuilder().copying(email)</code> keep these overrides, including recipient order and repeated occurrences.
			  Editing the copy's subject or body therefore keeps it routed to your testers. Use <code>clearOverrideReceivers()</code> on the copy's builder
			  to remove that routing choice. The ordinary recipients are used when no receiver override applies; configured defaults, overrides and locks
			  still apply.
		  </p>
	  </div>

	  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="small">// send the email to your testers
currentEmailBuilder.withOverrideReceivers(tester1Recipient, testGroupInboxRecipient);</code></pre>
	  </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-debug-logging" id="section-debug-logging" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Enable Jakarta Mail debug logging</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        By default Jakarta Mail's debug logging is turned off, but you can turn it on for more diagnostic info. You can also route the debug stream
        to stdout, stderr or SLF4J without touching the internal Session instance directly. SLF4J output uses the
        <code>org.simplejavamail.javamail.debug</code> logger.
      </p>
	  <p>
		  Use <code class="inline language-java">SessionDebugConfig.builder().withPrinter(PrintStream)</code> when the built-in targets do not fit. Simple Java Mail passes that
		  stream to Jakarta Mail but does not close it. Keep it open for as long as the Mailer may use it, then close it yourself.
	  </p>
	  <p>Use <code>SessionDebugConfig</code> or the Simple Java Mail properties shown below to configure debug logging.
        Setting <code>mail.debug</code> alone does not enable debugging for a Session created by Simple Java Mail.</p>
	  <p>If you supply your own Session, Simple Java Mail preserves its debug setting and output stream unless your Mailer
        configuration changes them.</p>
	  <p>
		  A custom <code class="inline language-java">PrintStream</code> is a Java-only setting: a live stream cannot be represented by a property or CLI
		  argument. For those forms, choose <code class="inline language-java">STDOUT</code>, <code class="inline language-java">STDERR</code>, or
		  <code class="inline language-java">SLF4J</code> through <code class="inline language-java">SessionDebugOutput</code> instead.
	  </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("host", 587, "username", "password")
    .withDebugConfig(SessionDebugConfig.builder().withEnabled(true).withOutput(SessionDebugOutput.SLF4J).build())
    .buildMailer();

// or
mailer.getSession().setDebug(true);

// or
yourSession.setDebug(true);
mail.mailerBuilder(yourSession);
</code></pre>
      or:
      <pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=true
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug.out=SLF4J
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
	  <p>
		  In this example both resources have a clear owner. Try-with-resources closes the Mailer first and the debug stream afterwards, so Jakarta Mail
		  cannot write to an already closed stream.
	  </p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (PrintStream jakartaMailDebug = new PrintStream(
        Files.newOutputStream(Paths.get("jakarta-mail-debug.log")));
     Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("host", 587, "username", "password")
        .withDebugConfig(SessionDebugConfig.builder().withEnabled(true).withPrinter(jakartaMailDebug).build())
        .buildMailer()) {

    mailer.sync().testConnection();
}
</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-smtp-capabilities" id="section-smtp-capabilities" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Inspect SMTP capabilities and TLS</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Your production relay advertises different capabilities from the one on your laptop. Probe the production endpoint to see
      whether STARTTLS completed and what the server advertised afterward. This opens a separate connection and sends no mail.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()
        .mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587)
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .buildMailer()) {
    SmtpConnectionReport report = mailer.sync().probeConnection();
    logger.info("SMTP connection:\n{}", report);

    report.getEffectiveCapabilities().ifPresent(capabilities -&gt; {
        logger.info("DSN advertised: {}", capabilities.supports("DSN"));
        Long maximum = capabilities.getMaximumMessageSize();
        if (maximum != null) {
            logger.info("Advertised SIZE limit: {} bytes", maximum);
        }
    });

    if (report.getFailurePhase() != null) {
        logger.warn("{}: {}", report.getFailurePhase(),
            report.getFailureDescription());
    }
}</code></pre>
    <h3>Read the STARTTLS result</h3>
<pre><code class="language-text small">SMTP connection probe: SUCCESS
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Greeting: 220 smtp.example.org ready
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760], STARTTLS=[]}
STARTTLS: completed
After TLS: {AUTH=[LOGIN PLAIN], DSN=[], SIZE=[26214400]}
TLS active during setup: true
TLS details: TLSv1.3, cipher=TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, peer=[CN=smtp.example.org, smtp.example.org], configured identity check=true, custom hostname verifier=not observed
Authentication: not requested
Elapsed: 138ms</code></pre>
    <p>In this report, STARTTLS completed, the SIZE limit increased from 10 MiB to 25 MiB, and DSN appeared after encryption.
      Use <code>getEffectiveCapabilities()</code> for the applicable snapshot. If that snapshot is absent, treat the server’s capabilities as unknown.
      From the CLI, use <a href="cli.html#section-probe"><code>sjm probe</code></a>.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Authenticate, probe asynchronously or use a supplied Session</summary>
<pre><code class="language-java small">// On a Mailer with credentials already configured:
SmtpConnectionReport withAuthentication = mailer.sync().probeConnection(true);
boolean authenticated = withAuthentication.isAuthenticated();

// Or run the probe in the background; keep the Mailer open until it finishes.
CompletableFuture&lt;SmtpConnectionReport&gt; pending =
    mailer.async().probeConnection();</code></pre>      <p>When you supply your own Session, set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> before calling <code>probeConnection()</code>.
        You can set it on the Session or use <code>mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.transport.protocol", "smtps")</code> for SMTPS.
        Without it, the probe returns an unsupported report explaining the missing setting and opens no connection. You can still send
        normally using your Session's selected transport. See <a href="understanding-configuration.html#supplied-session-protocol">protocol settings and conflicts</a>.</p>
      <p>
        The default probe does not authenticate with the SMTP server: it neither consults cached SMTP credentials nor calls your Jakarta Mail
        authenticator or OAuth2 token provider. Configured proxy authentication and TLS client certificates still apply.
        Pass <code>true</code> to test the configured credentials as well. This choice applies only to this probe; ordinary sending is unchanged.
        Authentication follows your existing TLS and AUTH settings, so check those before enabling it against an unfamiliar endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        The synchronous methods always run on the caller's thread. <code>mailer.async().probeConnection()</code> and
        <code>mailer.async().probeConnection(true)</code> use the Mailer's executor and complete after transport and proxy cleanup have been attempted.
        Execution mode is selected by the view, not by configuration. Connection failures become reports; scheduling and Mailer-shutdown failures
        complete an async future exceptionally.
      </p>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Interpreting capability snapshots and SIZE</summary>
<p>
        This illustrative report shows a server that advertises a different SIZE limit and adds DSN after STARTTLS.
        <code>getBeforeTls()</code> is history; <code>getAfterTls()</code> describes the encrypted EHLO. Use
        <code>getEffectiveCapabilities()</code> when you want the applicable snapshot. Failed connection setup, rejected post-TLS EHLO,
        HELO-only fallback, and oversized diagnostic replies can leave it empty. Empty means unknown, not &ldquo;no extensions supported.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getExtensions()</code> includes unknown extensions and repeated advertisements, in sorted name order.
        <code>supports("DSN")</code> is case-insensitive; the same lookup works for SMTPUTF8, 8BITMIME, PIPELINING, CHUNKING,
        BINARYMIME and REQUIRETLS. These are server advertisements, not claims that Simple Java Mail used or implemented those features.
        In particular, this probe adds no PIPELINING or CHUNKING send path.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getMaximumMessageSize()</code> only returns a consistent positive numeric SIZE limit. An absent limit, zero (no fixed maximum),
        conflicting duplicates and malformed values return empty. Check <code>supports("SIZE")</code> separately.
        A probe uses a separate connection. For an actual send, the managed Angus path checks its own current limit before submission and
        exposes measured content bytes and the server limit on the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-message-size">submission receipt</a>.
      </p>
</details>

  </div></section>

  <h3 id="section-smtp-evidence">Keep configuration, negotiation and delivery apart</h3>
  <section>
    <p>You set STARTTLS, but did this connection actually negotiate it? <code>mailer.getTransportStrategy()</code> shows the configured
      strategy. The probe report describes what happened on the connection it opened.</p>
    <figure class="doc-diagram mermaid" data-pagefind-ignore>
%%{init: { "flowchart": { "padding": 8, "nodeSpacing": 24, "rankSpacing": 32, "wrappingWidth": 140 } } }%%
flowchart TB
    accTitle: What each SMTP diagnostic tells you
    accDescr: You can probe a connection or send a message using the Mailer settings. The probe reports TLS and advertised SMTP features for its own connection. A submission receipt records what is known about relay acceptance. A later delivery report may describe what happened to each recipient.
    settings["&lt;b&gt;Mailer settings&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Transport strategy"]
    probe["&lt;b&gt;Probe report&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;TLS and EHLO&lt;br/&gt;on this connection"]
    submit["&lt;b&gt;Submission receipt&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Relay acceptance,&lt;br/&gt;if known"]
    delivery["&lt;b&gt;Delivery report&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Recipient outcome"]
    settings --&gt;|Probe a connection| probe
    settings --&gt;|Send a message| submit
    submit -.-&gt;|If reported| delivery
    </figure>
    <p>For a message you sent, check the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-get-receipt">submission receipt</a> for relay acceptance.
      If a <a href="/delivery-reports.html">delivery report</a> arrives later, it can tell you what happened to individual recipients.</p>

    <h4>Read a failed STARTTLS probe</h4>
    <p>Say your relay answers EHLO with a SIZE limit and never offers STARTTLS. Here are the lines to start with.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">SMTP connection probe: FAILED
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760]}
STARTTLS: not attempted
After TLS: not available
TLS active during setup: false
Failure at STARTTLS: STARTTLS did not complete.</code></pre>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Find where it stopped.</strong> If <code>isSuccessful()</code> is false, read <code>getFailurePhase()</code> and
        <code>getFailureDescription()</code>. Here, check that the host and port you selected offer STARTTLS.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the handshake.</strong> TLS never started here. On another attempt, <code>getTlsDetails()</code> may show a completed
        handshake even if the hostname verifier rejected the connection afterward.</li>
      <li><strong>Check which features you can use.</strong> The plain EHLO helps explain the failure. <code>getEffectiveCapabilities()</code> is empty
        here because the required TLS setup failed. A real send checks capabilities on its own connection and may use a different endpoint.</li>
    </ol>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Report fields and size limits</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Success and cleanup.</strong> <code>isSuccessful()</code> covers connection setup, requested authentication and cleanup.
          <code>isConnected()</code> tells you that setup completed. The probe has already closed its connection when it returns the report.</li>
        <li><strong>TLS fields.</strong> When the Angus hooks capture them, <code>getTlsDetails()</code> gives you the negotiated protocol,
          cipher and public certificate identities. The identity-check flag shows the configured policy. The custom verifier result shows
          what that verifier decided. Angus reports up to 32 certificate identities, including the subject, and warns if it omitted any.
          The limit applies only to the report.</li>
        <li><strong>EHLO snapshot.</strong> Each snapshot has a 64 KiB budget for escaped UTF-8 extension names, parameters and formatting.
          The budget applies to the whole snapshot, regardless of line length or count. If the snapshot is too large, the report omits it
          and adds a warning. Provider reads and connection setup continue as usual. A real send checks SIZE in the full reply on its own connection.</li>
        <li><strong>Log output.</strong> The report bounds display text and escapes control characters. It includes the endpoint and public server
          or certificate names and leaves out raw provider exceptions and AUTH exchanges.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Endpoints, timeouts and provider support</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Where it connects.</strong> The probe uses the Mailer's endpoint, proxy and TLS settings, even in logging-only mode.
          A pooled Mailer probes its configured endpoint. With supported Angus connections, the report uses the provider's effective port.
          For example, a supplied Session with <code>mail.smtp.port=0x9db</code> connects to and reports <code>2523</code>. An absent or
          unusable value falls back to the provider's protocol default.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing and cancellation.</strong> The provider timeouts apply. The mail-send deadline and mail-send observer apply to sends.
          The reported elapsed time starts when the probe begins running, after any executor queue wait. An in-flight probe continues after
          its future is cancelled.</li>
        <li><strong>Trust settings.</strong> The probe uses your trust settings, hostname checks and supplied socket factories. If you configured
          a verifier by class name, it still runs and the report leaves TLS details unavailable.</li>
        <li><strong>Provider support.</strong> The built-in adapter supports Angus SMTP and SMTPS. Other providers need a
          <code>SmtpConnectionProbeAdapter</code>. A missing adapter or a <code>CustomMailer</code> returns an unsupported report without
          opening a connection. The probe leaves the original Session properties and provider registration intact. Some adapters can confirm a
          connection and leave EHLO or TLS details unavailable.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <p>Try the
      <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/SmtpConnectionProbeDemoApp.java">loopback demo</a>
      to see a real report without sending mail. Use <code>--configured</code> to try the shared demo SMTP settings, and add
      <code>--authenticate</code> in that mode to test credentials.</p>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-diagnostics" id="section-config-diagnostics" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Trace loaded configuration values</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Your production worker connects to a different relay from the one in <code>application.properties</code>.
      Log the diagnostics from the <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory that built that worker’s Mailer.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">LOGGER.info("Resolved mail settings:\n{}", mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics());</code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-text">SMTP connection:
  simplejavamail.smtp.host = smtp.eu-west.example.org (source: application-production.properties)
  simplejavamail.smtp.password = &lt;redacted&gt; (source: systemEnvironment)
  simplejavamail.smtp.port = 2525 (source: commandLineArgs)</code></pre>
    <p>Here the production profile supplied the host and a command-line argument supplied the port. Follow the source on the line you care about.
      In Spring, that name comes from the underlying <code>PropertySource</code>, including when Spring resolves a placeholder.
      See <a href="/configuration.html#section-config-properties">source precedence</a> and <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring profiles</a>.</p>
    <p>If the factory loaded the expected value, compare it with <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code>.
      Java customization and a supplied Session enter at construction. See the
      <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#configuration-diagnostics">worked read-timeout example</a>.
      A <a href="understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">connection conflict</a> names the overlapping setting and its known sources.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Read redacted values and source names in Java</summary>
<pre><code class="language-java small">ConfigDiagnostics diagnostics =
    mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics();

for (ConfigDiagnosticGroup group : diagnostics.getGroups()) {
    System.out.println(group.getDisplayName());

    for (ConfigPropertyDiagnostic property
            : diagnostics.getProperties(group)) {
        System.out.printf(
            "  %s = %s (source: %s)%n",
            property.getPropertyName(),
            property.getDisplayValue(),
            property.getSourceName());
    }
}</code></pre>
</details>
    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary id="session-diagnostic-values">What is safe to log?</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>Factory diagnostics redact credentials, keys, certificates and suspicious extra properties. Ordinary config getters and <code>asMap()</code> expose actual values.</li>
        <li>Mailer and operational config summaries redact recognized secret properties. Application objects are described by type without calling their
          <code>toString()</code>. Entries with non-String keys are omitted. A returned property container is an inspection copy.</li>
        <li>The factory snapshot describes loaded properties. The operational snapshot describes the Mailer when built. Neither tracks live pools or transports.
          The SLF4J Session summary is formatted at DEBUG for <code>org.simplejavamail.mailer.internal.util.SessionLogger</code>.
          For an SMTP exchange use <a href="#section-debug-logging">Jakarta Mail protocol logging</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-config-diagnostics-demo" id="section-config-diagnostics-demo" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Mixed-source example</h3>

  <section><div class="wide">
      <p>
        The runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/ConfigDiagnosticsDemoApp.java">ConfigDiagnosticsDemoApp</a>
        builds a realistic source hierarchy from fake, in-memory properties and prints the resulting report. It does not build a Mailer or connect to an
        SMTP server. The output shown here is abridged; each <code class="inline language-text">...</code> only replaces more entries from the demo.
      </p>
    <p>The demo also shows how typed values are normalized, blank username or proxy-host replacements are handled,
      and control characters in a subject or source name are escaped.</p>
